Michigan has the most PFAS sites in the country, at least 192 as of May of 2019. Out of at least 610 known sites across the country. Last year, when the state tested public water systems serving nearly 80% of our residents, 10% of those systems, showed PFOS and that PFOS in the water in our homes, in our schools, and our workplaces showed up. In Melvindale in my district, PFAS just oozed out of the ground into the roadway. And residents are still searching for answers about where it came from. As construction began on the new international crossing, the Gordie Howe International Bridge, PFAS was found in the soil at the bridge site, in Delray neighborhood in Southwest Detroit, right next to the Detroit River, where drinking water is drawn from. This is a man-made crisis, and people like my residents back in the 13th district are the ones who suffer from it.
